{:edb/db-before-t 1763 :edb/db-after-t 1764 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535076 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:18:16.839Z" 13194139535076 true] [17592186087502 1100 "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/6191f617d2c2e89e07c1cc349a5926874f11ea78d046185ce990e566225a7c79.png)" 13194139535076 true] [17592186087502 1100 "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535076 false] [17592186087502 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." 13194139535076 true] [17592186087502 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/867/Source/Images/a075449f9d98c2f5b4e14b915f2365f2.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= T(i) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i \\\\\nj^{′} &= T(j) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} =-j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/867/Source/Images/45b1b054a2d140d9a815f9b4816138e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." 13194139535076 false] [17592186087503 1100 "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139535076 true] [17592186087503 1100 "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139535076 false] [17592186087503 1145 "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v) &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535076 false] [17592186087503 1145 "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)" 13194139535076 true] [17592186087688 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329920 13194139535076 true] [17592186087688 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329968 13194139535076 true] [17592186087688 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329975 13194139535076 true] [17592186087688 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329982 13194139535076 true] [17592186087689 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329927 13194139535076 true] [17592186087689 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329936 13194139535076 true] [17592186087689 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329945 13194139535076 true] [17592186087689 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329954 13194139535076 true] [17592186087689 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329961 13194139535076 true] [17592186088303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535076 true] [17592186088303 1145 "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329921 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1089 "q-12043" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1100 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1113 #uuid "0591c45f-50ff-5065-b712-d7946fc4e140" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329920 1145 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=12.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $12.$" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329922 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329923 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329924 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329925 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329926 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1041 "selection" 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329923 13194139535076 true] [17592186329921 1110 #uuid "f8eaee76-14e4-5f04-9c0